    content: `JavaScript is the programming language of the web. While HTML provides structure and CSS provides styling, JavaScript adds behavior — making pages interactive, dynamic, and connected to live data. It is the only programming language natively supported by all web browsers, which has made it one of the most widely used languages in the world.\n\nJavaScript was created by Brendan Eich at Netscape in just 10 days in 1995. Despite its rushed origins, it has evolved dramatically. Today's JavaScript (ECMAScript 2024) is a mature, powerful language capable of building everything from simple web page interactions to enterprise-scale web applications, mobile apps (React Native), desktop apps (Electron), and server-side systems (Node.js).\n\nWhat JavaScript can do in the browser:\n- Dynamically change HTML content: document.getElementById('title').textContent = 'Hello!';\n- Respond to user events: clicks, keystrokes, form submissions, scroll position\n- Fetch data from servers without reloading the page (AJAX / Fetch API)\n- Store data locally (localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB)\n- Draw graphics (Canvas API, WebGL)\n- Handle real-time communication (WebSockets)\n- Access device APIs: camera, microphone, geolocation, notifications\n\nAdding JavaScript to an HTML page:\n<!-- External file (best practice) -->\n<script src="app.js" defer></script>\n\n<!-- Inline script (avoid for large code) -->\n<script>\n  console.log('Hello, Web Developer!');\n</script>\n\nThe browser Console is your primary debugging tool. Open it with F12 (or Ctrl+Shift+I on Windows, Cmd+Option+I on Mac). The console.log() function outputs values there:\nconsole.log('Hello!');\nconsole.log(42);\nconsole.log([1, 2, 3]);\nconsole.error('Something went wrong!');\nconsole.warn('This is a warning');\nconsole.table([{ name: 'Alice', age: 25 }, { name: 'Bob', age: 30 }]);\n\nJavaScript code executes line by line, top to bottom (unless redirected by functions, loops, or asynchronous operations).\n\nComments:\n// This is a single-line comment\n/* This is a\n   multi-line comment */\n\nJavaScript is case-sensitive. myVariable and MyVariable are different. It uses Unicode, so variable names can include letters from any language (though ASCII is the convention).\n\nThe ECMAScript standard: JavaScript is standardized by ECMA International as ECMAScript. New versions are released annually (ES2015, ES2016, ..., ES2024). ES2015 (also called ES6) was the most significant update, introducing classes, arrow functions, Promises, destructuring, and modules. These features are now standard in professional code.`,
