    duration: "30 min",
    content: `Variables are containers for storing data values. In JavaScript, you declare variables using three keywords: var (legacy), let (modern, block-scoped), and const (modern, block-scoped, cannot be reassigned).\n\nvar — Legacy (avoid in modern code):\nvar name = 'Alice';\nvar age = 25;\nProblems with var: it is function-scoped (not block-scoped), it is hoisted to the top of its function, and it allows redeclaration. These behaviors cause subtle bugs that are hard to trace.\n\nlet — Modern, mutable:\nlet score = 0;\nlet message = 'Loading...';\nscore = 100; // Reassignment allowed\n\nconst — Modern, immutable binding:\nconst PI = 3.14159;\nconst MAX_RETRIES = 3;\nconst apiUrl = 'https://api.example.com';\n\nConst does NOT mean the value is completely immutable — it means the variable binding cannot be reassigned. Objects and arrays declared with const can still have their contents modified:\nconst user = { name: 'Alice', age: 25 };\nuser.name = 'Bob'; // This is fine — modifying the object\nuser = {}; // ERROR — cannot reassign the variable\n\nRule of thumb: Always use const by default. Use let when you know the value needs to be reassigned. Never use var.\n\nJavaScript has 8 data types:\n\nPrimitive types (immutable, stored by value):\n1. String: Text. Written with single quotes, double quotes, or backticks.\n   let greeting = 'Hello'; let name = "Cameroon"; let message = "Hi, " + name + "!";\n\n2. Number: All numbers (integers and floats). JavaScript has only one number type.\n   let count = 42; let price = 9.99; let negative = -5;\n   Special values: Infinity, -Infinity, NaN (Not a Number)\n\n3. BigInt: Integers larger than Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER (2⁵³-1).\n   let big = 9007199254740991n;\n\n4. Boolean: true or false only.\n   let isLoggedIn = false; let hasPermission = true;\n\n5. undefined: A variable that has been declared but not yet assigned a value.\n   let x; console.log(x); // undefined\n\n6. null: Intentional absence of a value. Explicitly set by the programmer.\n   let user = null; // User not loaded yet\n\n7. Symbol: A guaranteed unique value, used for object property keys.\n   const id = Symbol('id');\n\nReference types (mutable, stored by reference):\n8. Object: Everything that is not a primitive. Arrays, functions, dates, and plain objects are all Objects.\n   let person = { name: 'Marie', age: 22, city: 'Douala' };\n   let scores = [95, 87, 92, 78];\n\nType checking with typeof:\nconsole.log(typeof 42);         // \"number\"\nconsole.log(typeof 'hello');    // \"string\"\nconsole.log(typeof true);       // \"boolean\"\nconsole.log(typeof undefined);  // \"undefined\"\nconsole.log(typeof null);       // \"object\" — famous JavaScript quirk!\nconsole.log(typeof []);         // \"object\" — arrays are objects\nconsole.log(typeof {});         // \"object\"\nconsole.log(typeof function(){}); // \"function\"\n\nType coercion — JavaScript automatically converts types in certain contexts:\n'5' + 3 = '53' // String concatenation wins\n'5' - 3 = 2    // Numeric subtraction (string coerced to number)\n'5' * '3' = 15 // Both coerced to numbers\ntrue + 1 = 2   // true coerced to 1\nfalse + 1 = 1  // false coerced to 0`,
