    title: "Control Flow & Conditionals",
    type: "practical",
    duration: "30 min",
    content: "Control flow is the order in which instructions are executed. By default, code runs top to bottom. Conditionals allow code to branch — taking different paths based on conditions. This is the foundation of all decision-making logic in programming.\n\nif / else if / else:\nconst score = 78;\n\nif (score >= 90) {\n  console.log('Grade: A');\n} else if (score >= 80) {\n  console.log('Grade: B');\n} else if (score >= 70) {\n  console.log('Grade: C');\n} else if (score >= 60) {\n  console.log('Grade: D');\n} else {\n  console.log('Grade: F — Please retake');\n}\n\nJavaScript evaluates conditions as truthy or falsy. The following values are falsy:\n- false\n- 0 and -0\n- '' (empty string)\n- null\n- undefined\n- NaN\n\nEverything else is truthy — including '0' (non-empty string), [] (empty array), {} (empty object).\n\nif (user) { /* runs if user is any truthy value */ }\nif (!user) { /* runs if user is null, undefined, '', 0, false, NaN */ }\n\nswitch statement — for multi-way branches based on a single value:\nconst day = 'Monday';\n\nswitch (day) {\n  case 'Monday':\n  case 'Tuesday':\n  case 'Wednesday':\n  case 'Thursday':\n  case 'Friday':\n    console.log('Weekday');\n    break;\n  case 'Saturday':\n  case 'Sunday':\n    console.log('Weekend');\n    break;\n  default:\n    console.log('Unknown day');\n}\n\nThe break statement is crucial — without it, execution 'falls through' to the next case. Falling through intentionally (stacking cases without break) is valid, as shown above for grouping Monday-Friday.\n\nswitch uses strict equality (===) for comparisons.\n\nEarly Return Pattern — avoid deeply nested if/else:\n// Bad — deeply nested\nfunction processUser(user) {\n  if (user) {\n    if (user.isActive) {\n      if (user.hasPermission) {\n        // actual work here\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// Good — guard clauses with early return\nfunction processUser(user) {\n  if (!user) return;\n  if (!user.isActive) return;\n  if (!user.hasPermission) return;\n  // actual work here — clean and readable\n}\n\nConditional logic with logical operators (short-circuit):\nconst username = inputValue || 'Guest'; // If inputValue is falsy, use 'Guest'\nconst displayName = user?.name ?? 'Anonymous'; // null-safe with nullish coalescing\n\nisNaN() vs Number.isNaN():\n- isNaN('hello') → true (coerces to NaN)\n- Number.isNaN('hello') → false (no coercion — 'hello' is a string, not NaN)\n- Number.isNaN(NaN) → true\nPrefer Number.isNaN() for strict checking.",
