    content: "Loops allow you to execute a block of code repeatedly. They are essential for processing arrays, handling data collections, rendering lists, and any situation where you need to perform the same operation multiple times.\n\nfor loop — when you know the number of iterations:\nfor (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n  console.log('Iteration:', i); // 0, 1, 2, 3, 4\n}\n\nStructure: initialization ; condition ; update\n- Initialization (let i = 0): Runs once before the loop starts\n- Condition (i < 5): Checked before each iteration. Loop stops when false\n- Update (i++): Runs after each iteration\n\nfor...of — iterating over iterable values (arrays, strings, Maps, Sets):\nconst students = ['Marie', 'Emmanuel', 'Flore', 'Jean'];\nfor (const student of students) {\n  console.log(student);\n}\n\nfor...in — iterating over object keys (NOT recommended for arrays):\nconst profile = { name: 'Alice', age: 25, city: 'Yaounde' };\nfor (const key in profile) {\n  console.log(key, ':', profile[key]);\n}\n// name : Alice, age : 25, city : Yaounde\n\nwhile loop — when the number of iterations is unknown:\nlet attempts = 0;\nlet password = '';\nwhile (password !== 'secret123') {\n  password = prompt('Enter password:');\n  attempts++;\n  if (attempts > 3) break;\n}\n\ndo...while — executes at least once before checking condition:\nlet count = 0;\ndo {\n  console.log('Count:', count);\n  count++;\n} while (count < 3);\n\nLoop Control:\nbreak — exits the loop immediately:\nfor (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {\n  if (i === 5) break; // Exits when i reaches 5\n  console.log(i);\n}\n\ncontinue — skips the rest of the current iteration:\nfor (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {\n  if (i % 2 === 0) continue; // Skip even numbers\n  console.log(i); // Prints: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9\n}\n\nArray iteration methods (preferred over for loops for arrays):\nconst scores = [85, 92, 78, 96, 88];\n\n// forEach — execute a function for each element:\nscores.forEach((score, index) => {\n  console.log('Student ' + (index + 1) + ': ' + score);\n});\n\n// map — transform each element, returns new array:\nconst grades = scores.map(s => s >= 90 ? 'A' : s >= 80 ? 'B' : 'C');\n// ['B', 'A', 'C', 'A', 'B']\n\n// filter — return only elements matching condition:\nconst passing = scores.filter(s => s >= 80); // [85, 92, 96, 88]\n\n// find — return FIRST element matching condition:\nconst firstAbove90 = scores.find(s => s > 90); // 92\n\n// reduce — accumulate to a single value:\nconst total = scores.reduce((sum, s) => sum + s, 0); // 439\nconst average = total / scores.length; // 87.8\n\n// some — is at least one element true?\nconst anyFailing = scores.some(s => s < 60); // false\n\n// every — are all elements true?\nconst allPassing = scores.every(s => s >= 70); // true",
