    content: "Functions are reusable blocks of code that perform a specific task. They are the most important tool for organizing JavaScript code — they allow you to write code once and call it multiple times with different inputs.\n\nFunction Declaration:\nfunction greet(name) {\n  return 'Hello, ' + name + '!';\n}\nconsole.log(greet('Marie')); // 'Hello, Marie!'\n\nFunction declarations are hoisted — they are available before the line where they are defined:\nconsole.log(greet('Alice')); // Works! Function declarations are hoisted.\nfunction greet(name) { return 'Hello, ' + name; }\n\nFunction Expression:\nconst greet = function(name) {\n  return 'Hello, ' + name + '!';\n};\n\nFunction expressions are NOT hoisted — the variable is hoisted but not the function:\ngreet('Alice'); // TypeError: greet is not a function\nconst greet = function(name) { return 'Hello, ' + name; };\n\nArrow Functions (ES6) — concise syntax:\nconst add = (a, b) => a + b;\nconst double = n => n * 2; // Single param: no parentheses needed\nconst getUser = () => ({ name: 'Alice', age: 25 }); // Wrap object in ()\n\nArrow functions with a block body:\nconst processScore = (score) => {\n  if (score >= 90) return 'A';\n  if (score >= 80) return 'B';\n  return 'C';\n};\n\nKey differences between arrow functions and regular functions:\n1. Arrow functions have no own 'this' — they inherit 'this' from the enclosing scope\n2. Arrow functions cannot be used as constructors (no new keyword)\n3. Arrow functions have no 'arguments' object\n\nParameters and Arguments:\nfunction createUser(name, age = 18, role = 'student') {\n  // Default parameter values (age defaults to 18 if not provided)\n  return { name, age, role };\n}\ncreateUser('Bob', 22, 'admin'); // { name: 'Bob', age: 22, role: 'admin' }\ncreateUser('Alice'); // { name: 'Alice', age: 18, role: 'student' }\n\nRest Parameters — collect multiple arguments into an array:\nfunction sum(...numbers) {\n  return numbers.reduce((total, n) => total + n, 0);\n}\nsum(1, 2, 3, 4, 5); // 15\n\nScope — where variables are accessible:\n- Global scope: Declared outside any function or block. Accessible everywhere.\n- Function scope: var is accessible anywhere within the function.\n- Block scope: let and const are only accessible within the {} block they are declared in.\n\nClosure — a function that 'remembers' variables from its outer scope even after the outer function has returned:\nfunction createCounter() {\n  let count = 0; // This variable is captured by the closure\n  return {\n    increment: () => ++count,\n    decrement: () => --count,\n    getCount: () => count\n  };\n}\nconst counter = createCounter();\ncounter.increment(); // count becomes 1\ncounter.increment(); // count becomes 2\ncounter.getCount(); // 2\n// count is private — cannot be accessed directly\n\nClosures enable data privacy and are the foundation of module patterns, React hooks, and many advanced patterns.\n\nIIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression) — self-executing function:\n(function() {\n  // Code here runs immediately\n  // Variables inside are private to this scope\n}());",
