    content: "Arrays are ordered collections of values. They are the most commonly used data structure in JavaScript. Mastering arrays and their built-in methods is essential for handling data in web applications — displaying lists of students, filtering search results, sorting scores, and transforming API responses.\n\nCreating Arrays:\nconst empty = [];\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\nconst mixed = [42, 'hello', true, null, { name: 'Alice' }];\nconst nested = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]];\nconst fromConstructor = new Array(5); // [undefined x 5]\nconst filled = new Array(5).fill(0); // [0, 0, 0, 0, 0]\n\nAccessing & Modifying:\nconst fruits = ['mango', 'papaya', 'avocado', 'pineapple'];\nfruits[0]; // 'mango' (index from 0)\nfruits[fruits.length - 1]; // 'pineapple' (last element)\nfruits.at(-1); // 'pineapple' (ES2022 — negative indexing)\nfruits[1] = 'guava'; // Modify element\n\nAdding & Removing Elements:\nfruits.push('banana'); // Add to END — returns new length\nfruits.pop(); // Remove from END — returns removed element\nfruits.unshift('coconut'); // Add to BEGINNING — returns new length\nfruits.shift(); // Remove from BEGINNING — returns removed element\nfruits.splice(1, 1); // Remove 1 element at index 1\nfruits.splice(1, 0, 'lime'); // Insert 'lime' at index 1 (remove 0)\nfruits.splice(1, 1, 'lime'); // Replace element at index 1 with 'lime'\n\nSearching:\nfruits.indexOf('papaya'); // First index of 'papaya', or -1 if not found\nfruits.lastIndexOf('mango'); // Last index of 'mango'\nfruits.includes('avocado'); // true or false\nfruits.findIndex(f => f.startsWith('p')); // Index of first match\n\nArray manipulation (non-mutating — return new arrays):\nconst a = [1, 2, 3];\nconst b = [4, 5, 6];\na.concat(b); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\na.slice(1, 3); // [2, 3] — from index 1 (inclusive) to 3 (exclusive)\na.reverse(); // [3, 2, 1] — MUTATES original!\na.sort(); // Alphabetical sort — MUTATES original!\na.sort((x, y) => x - y); // Numeric ascending sort\na.sort((x, y) => y - x); // Numeric descending sort\n\nFlattening:\nconst nested = [1, [2, 3], [4, [5, 6]]];\nnested.flat(); // [1, 2, 3, 4, [5, 6]]\nnested.flat(2); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6] — 2 levels deep\nnested.flat(Infinity); // Completely flatten any depth\n\nArray.from() — convert iterables to arrays:\nArray.from('hello'); // ['h', 'e', 'l', 'l', 'o']\nArray.from({ length: 5 }, (_, i) => i); // [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]\nArray.from(new Set([1, 2, 2, 3])); // [1, 2, 3] — deduplicate!\n\nDestructuring:\nconst [first, second, ...rest] = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];\n// first=10, second=20, rest=[30,40,50]\n\nconst [x, , z] = [1, 2, 3]; // Skip second element\n// x=1, z=3",
