    id: 28,
    module: "Module 3: JavaScript Basics",
    title: "Objects & Properties",
    type: "theory",
    duration: "30 min",
    content: "Objects are the most fundamental data structure in JavaScript. Almost everything in JavaScript is an object — arrays, functions, dates, maps, and sets are all objects. Understanding objects thoroughly is essential for working with API data, building components, and structuring applications.\n\nObject Literal Syntax:\nconst student = {\n  name: 'Marie-Claire',\n  age: 22,\n  school: 'University of Buea',\n  region: 'Southwest',\n  isEnrolled: true,\n  scores: [85, 92, 78],\n  address: {\n    city: 'Buea',\n    country: 'Cameroon'\n  }\n};\n\nAccessing Properties:\nstudent.name; // 'Marie-Claire' — dot notation\nstudent['name']; // 'Marie-Claire' — bracket notation\nstudent['school']; // Bracket notation required for dynamic keys\nstudent.address.city; // Nested access: 'Buea'\n\nBracket notation is required when the property name is stored in a variable:\nconst key = 'name';\nstudent[key]; // 'Marie-Claire'\n\nAdding & Modifying Properties:\nstudent.email = 'marie@example.cm'; // Add new property\nstudent.age = 23; // Modify existing\ndelete student.isEnrolled; // Remove property\n\nObject Methods:\nconst calculator = {\n  value: 0,\n  add(n) { this.value += n; return this; }, // Chainable!\n  subtract(n) { this.value -= n; return this; },\n  result() { return this.value; }\n};\ncalculator.add(10).subtract(3).result(); // 7\n\n'this' inside a method refers to the object the method is called on.\n\nShorthand property names (when variable name = property name):\nconst name = 'Alice';\nconst age = 25;\nconst user = { name, age }; // Same as { name: name, age: age }\n\nDestructuring:\nconst { name, age, region = 'Unknown' } = student;\n// name='Marie-Claire', age=22, region='Southwest'\n// region='Unknown' is the default if property is undefined\n\nRenaming during destructuring:\nconst { name: studentName, age: studentAge } = student;\n// studentName='Marie-Claire', studentAge=22\n\nNested destructuring:\nconst { address: { city } } = student;\n// city='Buea'\n\nObject methods for inspection:\nObject.keys(student); // ['name', 'age', 'school', ...] — array of property names\nObject.values(student); // ['Marie-Claire', 22, 'University of Buea', ...]\nObject.entries(student); // [['name','Marie-Claire'], ['age',22], ...]\n\nObject.assign() — shallow copy / merge:\nconst defaults = { role: 'student', active: true };\nconst newUser = Object.assign({}, defaults, { name: 'Bob' });\n// { role: 'student', active: true, name: 'Bob' }\n\nSpread syntax for object copy/merge:\nconst updated = { ...student, age: 23, email: 'marie@new.cm' };\n// Creates new object with all student properties, but age=23 and new email\n\nChecking property existence:\n'name' in student; // true\nstudent.hasOwnProperty('name'); // true (not inherited)\nstudent.nonExistent === undefined; // true (but could be set to undefined intentionally)\n\nOptional chaining for safe nested access:\nstudent?.address?.city; // 'Buea' (no error if address is undefined)",
